    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#VM" source="#autopsy">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/>Ἐνγυμάχο
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text from autopsy</note>
                    </app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation">
                <p><!--translation(s) - add @xml:lang for 'en' or 'it' to div--></p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" resp="#VM">
                <p>The inscription can be dated on palaeographical grounds to the late sixth or early fifth century BCE . 
                    The trident-shaped chi, dotted alpha and curved gamma point to a Geloan-Akragantine alphabetic context. 
                    The text is read as Ἐνγυμάχο (= Ἐγγυμάχο), an otherwise unattested personal name in the genitive, 
                    and is therefore best interpreted as an ownership inscription. Its formation from ἐγγύς and μάχη 
                    may be compared with the name Ἐγγύδιχος.
                    The function of the object remains uncertain. The square recess and its three shallow holes may have 
                    accommodated a square-section handle secured by three projections or nails. 
                    Some Roman stone weights provide morphological parallels, but its weight of 187.392 g does not correspond 
                    to any known weight standard attested by comparable objects in Archaic or Classical Sicily. Alternatively, it may 
                    have been a small hafted tool, perhaps a hammer intended for a specific purpose. 
                    The absence of wear might be explained by a votive use, but the generic provenance from the Fusco 
                    area does not permit a more precise interpretation because of the lack of archaeological context.</p>
            </div>
